{"id": "48453ccf-1585-442f-967c-839e2bf06452", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T03:20:33.767478", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "4196264f-4cad-4706-8d95-5db785f4f7eb", "tool_name": "inspect_repository", "tool_call_id": "call-6b2f6840-6653-41f4-a585-ec69a253091c", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"question\": \"Incorporating the chemical feedback mechanism, which leverages properties such as p-logP, QED, and binding affinity, directly improves the generation of molecules with superior chemical properties and reduces molecular hallucinations in targeted molecule discovery tasks. Does embedding this feedback loop better align molecule generation with real-world distributions, preserving structural diversity (e.g., avoiding over-representation of ultra-long carbon chains) while enhancing property scores?\",\n  \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\",\n  \"documents\": {\n    \"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n  }\n}\n"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "51ab80b7-2f06-4383-bc5d-9d2499bf4618", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 8}
{"id": "996a20c3-24f4-4ac2-a5f5-6089130e210f", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T03:20:33.784033", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "48453ccf-1585-442f-967c-839e2bf06452", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-b5f7a610-e505-4f98-979e-5186734d1fec", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"ls -la /workspace && ls -la /\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.016, \"output\": \"total 8\\ndrwx------  2 1776737281 748400513 4096 Oct 23 10:20 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 1776737281 748400513  240 Oct 23 10:20 ..\\n-rw-------  1 1776737281 748400513  794 Oct 23 10:20 task.json\\ntotal 92\\ndrwxr-xr-x  12 1776737281 748400513   240 Oct 23 10:20 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x  12 1776737281 748400513   240 Oct 23 10:20 ..\\ndrwxr-xr-x   2      65534     65534 73728 Oct 22 23:59 bin\\ndrwxr-xr-x   4 1776737281 748400513   320 Oct 23 10:20 dev\\ndrwx------   2 1776737281 748400513    60 Oct 23 10:20 etc\\ndrwxr-xr-x   3 1776737281 748400513    60 Oct 23 10:20 home\\ndrwxr-xr-x 128      65534     65534  4096 Oct 15 13:11 lib\\ndrwxr-xr-x   2      65534     65534  4096 Sep 11 13:39 lib64\\ndr-xr-xr-x 636      65534     65534     0 Oct 23 10:20 proc\\ndrwxr-xr-x   2 1776737281 748400513    40 Oct 23 10:20 tmp\\ndrwxr-xr-x  13      65534     65534  4096 Sep 17  2025 usr\\ndrwx------   2 1776737281 748400513  4096 Oct 23 10:20 workspace\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "4196264f-4cad-4706-8d95-5db785f4f7eb", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 9}
{"id": "383e234d-cd0d-4f92-945b-78dd32869f40", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T03:21:29.907848", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "996a20c3-24f4-4ac2-a5f5-6089130e210f", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-364f2961-39b5-4200-8fa8-f5ef5d9868cd", "tool_call": {"id": "call-364f2961-39b5-4200-8fa8-f5ef5d9868cd", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Recon plan: inputs, approach, open questions\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"\\n# Reconnaissance & proposed approach (PLAN – awaiting your approval)\\n\\n## 1. Inputs inventory\\n\\n| Item | Status | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `task.json` | Confirmed (read) | Contains the question, context, and a `documents.track.md` entry whose body is just one line: *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"* |\\n| `track.md` as a separate file | Not present in `/workspace` | Only referenced inside `task.json`. Treat its content as the verbatim directive above. |\\n| Researcher topics | Empty list (`researcher_topics: []`) | No pre-seeded clarifications — I have to ask. |\\n| Datasets, weights, GPU, pre-installed deps | None provided | `/workspace` is empty except for `task.json`. Network is disabled, 24 CPU cores, system Python only. No model weights downloadable in this env. |\\n| Public benchmarks to lean on | Available as known literature numbers, not as our results | MOSES paper baselines (e.g., MolDQN, REINVENT validity ≈ 95–98 %), GuacaMol benchmark, Olivecrona et al. 2017 for REINVENT, Blaschke et al. 2020 for REINVENT 2.0, Popova et al. 2019/2018 for JT-VAE/GraphAF, Jin et al. 2018/2020 JT-VAE, Brown et al. GuacaMol, Méndez-Lucio et al. 2020 for ChemSpace/conditional generation, Polykovskiy et al. 2020 MOSES, etc. |\\n\\n**What I need from you to firm the plan** is enumerated in §4. Everything below §2 is conditional on those answers.\\n\\n## 2. Proposed approach (high-level, design-only)\\n\\n### 2.1 Target task & conditioning format\\n- **Task type:** targeted ligand discovery — generate SMILES conditioned on a target and desired chemistry profile.\\n- **Default conditioning signal I'd propose (pending §4.Q6):** *target-conditioned via a 1-D protein embedding* (e.g., a frozen sequence token embedding from a pretrained ProtBERT / ESM-2) concatenated to the decoder state, **plus** a single \\\"warhead\\\" substructure seed supplied as SMILES, à la Méndez-Lucio et al. 2020 / Lim et al. 2020. Falls back to unconditional SMILES generation if you want the simpler ARM.\\n- **Benchmark targets:** DRD2, JNK3, GSK3β as de facto standards. DRD2 vs DRD3 selectivity is an extra eval signal, not a separate arm unless you want it.\\n\\n### 2.2 Base generator — to be confirmed\\nMy recommendation: **REINVENT-style SMILES-LM (RNN + attention), Priors & policies à la Olivecrona 2017 / Blaschke 2020**, augmented with the MCS-conditioned decoder head from Lim et al. 2020 for the target arm.\\n\\nWhy over graph generators (JT-VAE / GraphAF / DiffuMol):\\n- Mature toolchain for multi-property REINFORCE with a deterministic differentiable reward (likelihood-ratio, weighted-sum reward — the \\\"augmented likelihood\\\" of REINVENT 3.0).\\n- Rejection sampling is trivial to bolt on.\\n- Graph generative models either (a) need score-matching on continuous features (DiffuMol) which is awkward for discrete QED/p-logP/affinity signals, or (b) need tree/fragment actions that complicate partial-credit REINFORCE.\\n\\nIf you'd rather pick a graph generator, say the word and I'll redesign the RL loop around STONED / MoFlow / GraphAF.\\n\\n### 2.3 Feedback-loop mechanism\\n**Recommended: rejection-sampling warm-up → REINFORCE with weighted-sum reward (the REINVENT 3.0 \\\"augmented likelihood\\\").**\\n1. **Warm-up N1 batches** of rejection sampling from the prior, scoring each molecule with a weighted sum of property oracle calls; keep top-k to fine-tune the prior (SST — \\\"sampling spoil-transfer\\\"). Cheaper, lower-variance than a pure-RL cold start.\\n2. **Policy gradient phase:** per-token REINFORCE with an \\\"augmented likelihood\\\" loss that adds a small scalar σ·S(m) per molecule (S = reward, σ like 60–120 in published settings). Experience replay / learning rate cliffs à la Blaschke 2020 to avoid catastrophic forgetting.\\n3. **Diversity-preservation term:** add to the reward an internal-diversity bonus *across the sampled batch* (mean pairwise 1−Tanimoto) and an explicit **alkane-chain penalty** (any molecule with >K contiguous sp3 carbons, K to be set in §4).\\n\\nRationale vs. the alternatives: pure rejection sampling throws away compute and saturates at the prior's modifiable set; pure REINFORCE on sparse 3-term reward has high variance; constrained decoding (e.g., constrained tree decode) can't enforce QED/affinity without an expensive search oracle per token.\\n\\n### 2.4 Datasets — preview\\n- **Pre-training (language prior):** MOSES (≈1.6 M SMILES, already canonicalized) **or** a 1.5 M ChEMBL-28 random sample. MOSES gives us the public validity/uniqueness/novelty baseline numbers; ChEMBL aligns better with \\\"real-world drug-like\\\" distribution.\\n- **Distribution-learning eval:** MOSES test set + GuacaMol 10 K test (rediscovery / similarity /isomers /median molecules).\\n- **Target-conditioning fine-tuning:** ChEMBL28 DRD2 / JNK3 / GSK3β subsets (actives at standard cutoffs — typically IC50/Ki ≤ 1 µM, see §4) + matched decoys (DUD-E if we can name them; otherwise ChEMBL-decoys from same target with Ki > 10 µM). \\n- **Binding-affinity ground truth (held-out, not for RL training):** PDBbind-refined subset for evaluation of the affinity oracle only.\\n- *No code/datasets are touched in this design-only task — these are dataset selections to be approved, not claims of having downloaded them.*\\n\\n### 2.5 Metrics — three groups\\n\\n**(a) Chemical quality**\\n- Validity (canonical SMILES parses with RDKit).\\n- Uniqueness (unique among generated set; also reported @1 k / @10 k).\\n- Novelty (1 − max Tanimoto to training set, threshold 0.4 for \\\"novel\\\" per MOSES convention).\\n- Internal validity violations: valency errors, kekulization failures, large-ring (≥8) sanity.\\n- Substructure realism: KL between BRICS-fragment histograms of generated vs training set; also PAINS / Brenk alert counts.\\n\\n**(b) Property scores (the feedback signals themselves, plus independent re-scoring)**\\n- **QED** (Bickerton 2012) via RDKit.\\n- **Penalized logP** = logP − SA − ringP, with SA from Ertl's SA score, ringP max(0, 6 − rings)·logP sigmoid-like; includes explicit long-alkane penalty in §2.3 (this is the \\\"avoid overlong chains\\\" criterion you mentioned).\\n- **Binding affinity surrogate** — see §4.Q3 for choice. We will *re-score* the final generated set with a **different** held-out scorer (e.g., if RL used an ECFP-RF surrogate, re-score with a graph CNN or simple Vina on a stratified subsample) to detect surrogate overfitting.\\n\\n**(c) Structural diversity / hallucination indicators**\\n- Internal diversity = mean pairwise 1 − Tani(Morgan r=2, 2048 b).\\n- Nearest-neighbour Tanimoto to training (mean, 10th percentile).\\n- \\\"Ultra-long carbon chain\\\" fraction: σ-Patterson–style, count of generated molecules with contiguous sp3-C chain length ≥ 8 (threshold to confirm in §4).\\n- Scaffold entropy (Murcko scaffolds) and # unique scaffolds.\\n- Ring-size histogram (3-, 4-, ≥8-membered fractions).\\n- Hallucination proxy: % generated molecules with >0 BRICS fragments not seen in training; % with novel Murcko scaffolds not seen in training (target: *higher* than baseline only if scaffolds are useful, capped).\\n\\n### 2.6 Arms — controls and ablations\\n\\n| Arm | Feedback components |\\n|---|---|\\n| **A0** | base generator, no property feedback (prior only) |\\n| **A1** | + QED only |\\n| **A2** | + p-logP only (with SA + ringP + long-alkane penalty) |\\n| **A3** | + binding-affinity surrogate only |\\n| **A4** | + QED + p-logP + affinity (equal/tunable weights — see §4.Q1) |\\n| **A5** | A4 + explicit diversity bonus in reward (tests whether diversity degrades under feedback) |\\n| **A6** | **Random-feedback control**: same generator, deterministic RM scorer resampled; permutes molecule→score mapping inside each batch so any drift over A0 must come from true signal, not gradient noise — required to attribute gains to *real* property gradients (cf. olivecrona-type control in Blaschke 2020) |\\n| **A7 (optional)** | A4 with a *different* affinity oracle (e.g., Vina/RF) than the one used in A3 — sanity check that A3's gain is not oracle artefact |\\n\\nBudget per arm: 10 k generated molecules for evaluation + ~1 k–10 k policy updates for training (range to finalize in §4.Q4).\\n\\n## 3. Required controls beyond ablations\\n- Fixed random seeds across arms (seed = {0,1,2} for reproducibility bands).\\n- Identical training set, identical evaluator, identical re-scoring protocol for every arm.\\n- Held-out PDBbind + held-out ChEMBL target panel for *independent* binding evaluation.\\n- A \\\"stash\\\" of pre-feedback generator samples kept so A5's diversity claims can be remeasured against the unconditioned distribution.\\n\\n## 4. Open questions (please confirm before approve_approach)\\n\\n1. **Reward composition.** Only QED, p-logP, affinity in the feedback (no SA, no ringP separately)? Equal weights in the reward, or do you have a weighting scheme (e.g., 1·QED + 0.5·norm(p-logP) + 1·tanh(affinity))? Should the long-alkane penalty be additive inside p-logP, or a separate term?\\n2. **Target choice & bioactivity threshold.** Just DRD2 / JNK3 / GSK3β, or do you want DRD2-vs-DRD3 selectivity as a fourth signal? ChEMBL cutoff for \\\"active\\\" — 1 µM IC50/Ki, 100 nM, or 10 nM? Per-target decoys from DUD-E or random ChEMBL?\\n3. **Affinity oracle inside the RL loop.** Options: (i) Vina docking with cached pose — slowest, most realistic; (ii) RF regressor on Morgan/ECFP trained on ChEMBL/PDBbind — fast, risks surrogate overfitting; (iii) pretrained GNN affinity model (e.g., DimeNet / SchNet / a published AffinityNet) — moderate cost, moderate bias. My default would be (ii) RF-ECFP trained per target, with A7 using (iii) for sanity check.\\n4. **Generation budget per arm.** Is 10 k generated molecules / arm acceptable, with ~5 k policy-gradient updates during the REINFORCE phase? Some generative papers use 100 k–1 M — please confirm scale so I size the audit (re-eval, replay buffer, logging) accordingly.\\n5. **Score granularity.** Per-molecule scalar at end of trajectory (REINVENT-vanilla) vs. per-step token contribution (distributional RL)? I propose per-molecule — lower variance, matches REINVENT 3.0 reports.\\n6. **Conditioning.** Target-conditioned (sequence embedding + MCS scaffold seed) or unconditional? The question explicitly mentions \\\"targeted molecule discovery\\\", which suggests yes; confirm whether scaffold conditioning is *required* or only target-conditioned.\\n\\n## 5. Evidence I am leaning on (as known public benchmarks, not as our outcomes)\\n- MOSES public paper (Polykovskiy et al., 2020) — validity/uniqueness/novelty reference protocol.\\n- GuacaMol benchmark (Brown et al., 2019) — distribution-learning splits.\\n- REINVENT (Olivecrona et al. 2017) & REINVENT 2.0/3.0 (Blaschke et al. 2020) — augmented-likelihood REINFORCE + SST.\\n- JT-VAE (Jin et al. 2018), GraphAF (Shi et al. 2020), MoFlow (Zang & Wang 2020), DiffuMol (Xu et al. 2022) — graph-generator alternatives I considered.\\n- Conditional DGMs: Lim et al. 2020 (scaffold hopper), Méndez-Lucio et al. 2020 (ChemSpace).\\n- Penalized logP: Kusner et al. 2017 (GrammarVAE); SA score Ertl & Schuffenhauer 2009.\\n- DRD2/JNK3/GSK3β as De Novo Design benchmarks: Olivecrona 2017 / Blaschke 2020 / Brown 2019.\\n- DUD-E (Mysinger et al. 2012) for target-specific decoys.\\n\\nThese are cited as known reference points to anchor what \\\"good\\\" looks like, *not* as results we will report.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Ask:** Please answer §4's six questions (and confirm the generator + feedback-loop choices) before I move to `stage=result` with the full design document.\\n\",\"evidence\":\"\\nRead /workspace/task.json (only file present). Confirmed:\\n- The provided `documents.track.md` field contains a single line: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No separate track.md file exists in /workspace.\\n- `/workspace` directory contains only task.json. No data, code, model weights, or configs shipped.\\n- `researcher_topics` is `[]` — no pre-seeded clarifications.\\n- Environment: 24 vCPU, no GPU, system Python only, network disabled, no preinstalled deps.\\n\\nAll other \\\"evidence\\\" cited in this plan (benchmark numbers, architectures, datasets) is treated as background from public literature, not as project artefacts. Nothing in the repo, nothing executed. No claims of having run any experiment.\\n\",\"assumptions\":\"\\n- A1. Three feedback signals (QED, p-logP, binding affinity) are the intended reward components; SA score and ringP enter as *parts* of p-logP (Kusner/Ertl convention) and the long-alkane penalty is housed within p-logP unless you say otherwise.\\n- A2. Targeting DRD2, JNK3, GSK3β as the primary benchmark proteins (literature-standard for de novo design).\\n- A3. The base generator is a SMILES-LM with a transferable prior (REINVENT-style), fine-tuned to a target-conditioned policy via augmented-likelihood REINFORCE.\\n- A4. Affinity oracle in the RL loop is a per-target ECFP-RF regressor trained on ChEMBL/PDBbind; a *different* scorer is used for held-out re-scoring to detect surrogate overfitting.\\n- A5. Generation budget on the order of 10 k molecules per arm for evaluation; ~5 k policy updates per arm for the REINFORCE phase. These are sizes to confirm, not consumptions of compute.\\n- A6. Conditioning format = protein sequence embedding (frozen ProtBERT/ESM-2) + MCS-conditioned decoder seed