{"id": "3c997570-08d4-4eab-b378-30f542570d64", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T03:26:08.292645", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "34b07324-2792-4a0f-83b2-4ecb8dec12e4", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "\n# Full experiment design — Chemical feedback loop in targeted molecule discovery\n*Design-only; no experiment executed. All numerical anchors are public-literature references, not results.*\n\n---\n\n## 0. Pre-registered research questions & hypotheses\n\n**RQ.** Does an explicit feedback loop — QED + penalized logP + binding-affinity surrogate — in generator fine-tuning:\n- (a) improve property scores on the held-out evaluator,\n- (b) reduce \"molecular hallucinations\" (notably ultra-long sp3-C chains, valency errors, generation collapse to top-K scaffolds),\n- (c) preserve structural diversity relative to the unconditional prior.\n\n**H1 (property lift).** A4 (full triplet) attains higher held-out QED, p-logP and affinity than A0 (prior only) with p < 0.05 across 3 seeds.\n**H2 (hallucination reduction).** A4 reduces % molecules with ≥ 8 contiguous sp3-C and % invalid SMILES vs A0.\n**H3 (diversity preserved).** A5 (A4 + diversity bonus) is non-inferior to A4 on internal diversity; A4 + diversity bonus is non-inferior to A0 on internal diversity and unique-Murcko-scaffold count.\n**H4 (signal specificity).** A6 (random-feedback) shows no significant improvement over A0 on any property — establishes that gains are signal-driven, not noise-driven.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Base generator architecture\n\n**Choice (confirmed): REINVENT-style SMILES-LM** — RNN/Self-attention encoder–decoder over canonical SMILES with start/stop tokens, ~3–6 M parameters (range consistent with Olivecrona 2017 / Blaschke 2020 prior networks).\n\n- **Prior π_prior.** Pre-trained on MOSES 1.59 M SMILES (string-only prior, no properties) — used by all arms.\n- **Policy π_θ.** Initialized from π_prior; updated by either rejection warm-up (§3.1) or REINFORCE (§3.2).\n- **Tokenization.** Atom-wise SMILES with a small atom-vocab + ring-bond markers; same vocab as Prior; max length 128 tokens (MOSES convention; 99th-percentile of training set is ≈ 74 tokens).\n- **Chemistry validity is not enforced at decode time** — the feedback signal is the only validity pressure (so we can measure the hallucination frequency).\n\nWhy over JT-VAE / GraphAF / DiffuMol: REINVENT-style aug-likelihood REINFORCE accepts per-molecule scalar rewards natively, supports MCS-conditioned seeding, and has the most mature public reporting for the same targets (DRD2/JNK3/GSK3β) — direct comparability with public benchmarks.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Feedback signals — exact formulations\n\nAll three signals are computed per generated SMILES and aggregated into a scalar reward S(m).\n\n### 2.1 QED\nCompute with RDKit `QED.qed(m)` (Bickerton et al. 2012). Default range [0, 1].\n\n### 2.2 Penalized logP (with embedded long-alkane penalty, no extra weight)\nPer researcher direction (Q1): long-alkane penalty is additive *inside* p-logP, alongside the SA and ringP terms.\n\n```\npLogP(m) = clip(logP_Crippen(m), -5, +10)\n         - SA(m)                                          # Ertl & Schuffenhauer, 2009\n         - max(0, 6 - n_rings(m))                         # ring penalty, Kusner 2017\n         - alkane_penalty(m, K=8, beta=1.0)               # NEW: contiguous sp3-C >= K\n```\n\nWhere `alkane_penalty(m, K, β) = β · max(0, L_longest_sp3_C_chain(m) − K + 1)`. With K=8 the penalty ramps linearly for chain lengths ≥ 9.\n\nThe composite p-logP is normalised into [0,1] by `norm_pLogP = (pLogP − (-5)) / (10 − (-5))`-then-clipped; this matches `Q1`'s call for `norm(p-logP in [-5,+10])`.\n\n### 2.3 Binding-affinity surrogate\nPer-target **RF-ECFP** regressor pKi:\n- Features: Morgan ECFP, radius 2, 2048 bits, byte-packed.\n- Model: `sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=1000, n_jobs=-1, max_features=\"sqrt\", random_state=seed)` — per researcher Q3.\n- Training data per target: ChEMBL actives (IC50/Ki ≤ 1 µM, REINVENT-2.0 convention) ∪ DUD-E decoys for that target, both refolded to pKi (= 9 − log10(IC50_M)); 80/10/10 split; reported RMSE on 10 % holdout and held-back re-scoring set.\n- Surrogate output: `aff_pred = clip(pKi/10, 0, 1)` (pKi ∈ [0,10] → [0,1]).\n- Surrogate score passed into the reward: `tanh(3 · aff_pred)` for finite-range robustness (so a saturated hit does not indefinitely dominate the gradient).\n\n### 2.4 Composite reward (researcher-confirmed default)\n```\nS_default(m) = 0.5 · QED(m)\n             + 0.5 · norm_pLogP(m)\n             + 0.8 · tanh(3 · aff_pred(m))\n```\nRange S ∈ [0, ~2.1]; not normalised — REINVENT aug-likelihood operates on raw S with a `sigma` scaler (§3.2).\n\nFor A5 the diversity bonus `λ_div · (1/|B|) · Σ_{i≠j in B}(1 − Tani(i,j))` is added inside S (see §3.3).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Feedback-loop mechanism\n\n### 3.1 Rejection-sampling warm-up (1 k molecules by researcher budget)\n- Sample 100 000 SMILES from π_prior with T=1.0; canonicalize with RDKit.\n- Compute S_default(m) for each.\n- Keep top-k (k = 1 000 by default), and use these as *teacher data* for one-shot supervised fine-tuning of π_θ (cross-entropy on token sequences). This is SST — REINVENT 2.0 / Blaschke 2020.\n- Goal: move π_θ away from the prior enough that the policy-gradient phase has nonzero reward variance without diverging.\n\n### 3.2 REINFORCE with augmented likelihood (2–5 k updates by researcher budget)\n- Sample a batch of B = 256 SMILES from π_θ (T=1.0) at each step.\n- For each valid SMILES m, compute S(m). Invalid SMILES receive S = S_invalid (configurable but set to 0 by default).\n- Augmented-likelihood loss per step (REINVENT 3.0):\n\n```\nL(θ) = -E_{m ~ π_θ}[ Σ_t log π_θ(a_t | a_<t, cond)  ·  cloze_factor   ]\n        - σ · S(m)\n```\n\nwhere σ is a learned scaler (REINVENT-style scalar \"sigma\", initialised at 60–120; updated via a moving-average of |L| to keep the gradient contributions balanced). `cloze_factor` follows Blaschke 2020 (down-weights tokens completed long after average length to avoid bias toward short molecules).\n\n- Experience-replay buffer (§5) feeds 25 % of each batch from replay and 75 % from fresh sampling.\n- LR scheduler: linear warm-up 1 k steps, cosine decay to 10 % by step 5 k. σ updates every 50 steps.\n\n### 3.3 Diversity-preservation term (A5 only)\nAt REINFORCE step end, form S'(m) by adding to S_default(m) a per-batch diversity bonus:\n\n```\nS_A5(m) = S_default(m) + λ_div · (1/−Tani_vs_batch_mean(m))\n```\n\n`λ_div` initial value = 0.2, annealed linearly to 0.05 by step 5 k. Diversity is computed over the *batch* (cheap; per-batch, not per-experience). Effect: discourages within-batch collapse to identical Murcko scaffolds.\n\n### 3.4 Why not pure rejection sampling, constrained decoding, or distributional RL?\n- *Pure RS* (no REINFORCE): saturates at π_prior's modifiable support, weak DRD2-conditioning signal because the prior already produces drug-like molecules with random target affinity.\n- *Constrained decoding* (e.g., on scaffold alone): cannot enforce QED/affinity at per-token granularity without an expensive search oracle per token.\n- *Distributional RL / per-token credit:* increases variance for a relatively short trajectory (≤ 128 tokens), and we already have scaffold seed + augment-likelihood smoothing; user direction (Q5) is per-molecule scalar.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Datasets & conditioning\n\n| Role | Source | Size | Use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pre-training | MOSES (Polykovskiy 2020) | 1.59 M SMILES | π_prior |\n| Distribution eval | MOSES test + GuacaMol 10 K | ~120 k + 10 k | validity/uniqueness/novelty @1 k & @10 k |\n| Target fine-tune | ChEMBL28 filtered: DRD2 (CHEMBL217), JNK3 (CHEMBL1936), GSK3β (CHEMBL262) | IC50/Ki ≤ 1 µM | per-target RF training + policy-conditioning head |\n| Target decoys | DUD-E for the same three targets | DUD-E provided | per-target RF training (1:1 active:decoy) |\n| Affinity ground-truth refr. | PDBbind-refined 2020 | ≈ 5 K complexes | held-out surrogate re-fitter (NOT used to train RF in RL loop — only to sanity-check A7) |\n| Conditioning embedding | ESM-2 (650 M params, frozen) over UniProt sequences P14416 (DRD2), P52333 (JNK3 / MAPK10), P49841 (GSK3β) | 3 embeddings | injected into decoder init for A1–A6 |\n| Scaffold seeds | One known ChEMBL active per target, fixed across arms | 3 seeds | implanted as required substructure by decoder mask |\n\n**A0 — unconditional** (no conditioning embedding, no scaffold seed).\n**A1–A6 — target-conditioned** with the same per-target active seed (one molecule per target, chosen for being a strong literature active, used identically across seeds and arms so diversity claims are not confounded by scaffold choice).\n**A7 — re-scoring only**, not a training arm.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Hyperparameters & compute footprint\n\n### 5.1 Per-arm hyperparameters (frozen across seeds)\n| Param | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Batch size B | 256 |\n| Warm-up size | 100 000 sampled → 1 000 kept |\n| REINFORCE updates | 2 000 (sensitivity sweep) / 5 000 (main grid); capped |\n| Optimizer | Adam, lr = 1e-4 (warm-up); 5e-5 (REINFORCE) |\n| σ (REINVENT scaler) | init 100, EMA update β=0.95 |\n| Replay buffer size | 5 000 molecules (ring buffer; refresh policy below) |\n| Replay sampling fraction | 25 % per batch |\n| Replay refresh | when buffer full: keep the 80 % with highest σ·S among incoming, evict the rest (MoriaRE-style) |\n| Aug-likelihood cloze factor | linear, 0.5 at t=T/2, 1.0 at t=T |\n| Target seed for training subset | fixed (per target, opt-in by user) |\n\n### 5.2 Compute footprint\n**In the present sandbox (24 vCPU, no GPU, network disabled):** *the experiment cannot be run as defined.* Reasons: SMILES-LM training and ESM-2 inference benefit materially from a GPU; both the MOSES pre-training and ChEMBL filtering require network I/O not available. The sandbox is therefore used only for design — pre-registered specs, dry-run scripts, and unit-level probes (e.g., reward-function sanity tests) where possible.\n\n**CPU-only footprint estimate (per arm × per seed), if executed as a CPU-only feasibility prep:**\n- π_prior (frozen, no training): ~0 CPU-h (just loading weights).\n- Surrogate RF training: ~10–20 CPU-h per target (randomforest 1000 trees, 2048-bit features, ~10–30 K training rows).\n- Surrogate inference over 100 K+10 K SMILES per arm: ~3 CPU-h.\n- RDKit property pipeline (QED/SA/p-logP/alkane scan): ~1 CPU-h.\n- ESM-2 embedding of 3 targets (frozen): trivial, ~0.1 CPU-h.\n- REINVENT REINFORCE loop (5 000 updates × batch 256): **GPU-bound**, CPU-only estimate ≈ 30–50 CPU-h per arm × seed at ~1 batch / 30 s; reproduce-without-GPU would be impractically slow (≈ 2 weeks wall-clock per single arm-seed-target on a 24-vCPU box).\n- Evaluation: ~0.2 CPU-h per 10 K SMILES re-scoring.\n\n**Equivalent GPU footprint** (researcher-relevant, for a single A100/40 GB):\n- π_prior fine-tune / SST warm-up: ~30 min.\n- REINFORCE: ~6–10 h per arm × seed-target on A100.\n- Surrogate & property pipelines: trivial.\n- **Aggregate grid** = 3 targets × 7 arms × 3 seeds + 3 sensitivity points (DRD2 only) + A7 re-scoring overhead = **72 REINVENT runs × ~10 GPU-h ≈ 720 GPU-h ≈ 1 A100-month.** Plus pre-training (~1 A100-day if retraining, ~0 A100-day if released checkpoints are network-loaded — only the latter is realistic in a CPU+offline sandbox).\n- Storage: ~30 GB weights + ~10 GB datasets.\n\n### 5.3 Replay buffer\nRing buffer of 5 000 molecules (per arm). Refresh policy: when buffer full, score incoming molecules with σ·S, replace the lowest-scoring quartile. Sampling: 25 % of every batch drawn uniformly from the buffer, 75 % drawn from the current π_θ. Buffer state is *not* shared across arms (each arm keeps its own).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Arms (controls & ablations)\n\n| Arm | Feedback | Conditioning | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A0** | none | unconditional | prior-only samples, baseline for property/diversity |\n| **A1** | QED | target-conditioned | single-property isolation |\n| **A2** | p-logP (with embedded alkane penalty) | target-conditioned | isolates QED-vs-p-logP contribution |\n| **A3** | affinity (per-target RF-ECFP) | target-conditioned | isolates affinity contribution |\n| **A4** | QED + p-logP + affinity | target-conditioned | **full triplet**, default weights 0.5/0.5/0.8 |\n| **A5** | A4 + diversity bonus λ_div·pair_div | target-conditioned | tests whether diversity degrades under feedback |\n| **A6** | **random feedback** — molecule→score mapping permuted inside every batch (deterministic permutation seeded by iteration) | target-conditioned | gradient noise control (cf. Blaschke 2020); must remain flat vs A0 |\n| **A7** | re-scoring only (no training) | — | A3/A4 re-scored by an **out-of-loop GNN affinity scorer** (DimeNet-based or equivalent, public PyG implementation, frozen) on a stratified subsample of ≈ 200 molecules per (arm, target, seed); measures surrogate–out-of-distribution gap |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Subset ordering & execution schedule\n\nExecution is *embarrassingly parallel across arms × seeds × targets* but is sequenced to keep the audit matrix contiguous:\n\n- **Phase S — sensitivity sweep on DRD2 only** (single seed = 0 to keep cost low):\n  - weights `flat {1, 1, 1}` (s_A), `default {0.5, 0.5, 0.8}` (s_B), `affinity-heavy {0.3, 0.3, 1.0}` (s_C)\n  - applies **only to A4**; variants s_A, s_B, s_C\n- **Phase G — main grid:**\n  - 3 targets × 7 arms (A0–A6) × 3 seeds = **63 runs**\n- **Phase R — sanity / A7 re-scoring:**\n  - 3 targets × 2 arms (A3, A4) × 3 seeds = **18 runs** of re-scoring only\n- Total runs: **3 (sweep) + 63 (grid) + 18 (A7) = 84 runs**.\n\nRun order: interleaved by `(seed, target, arm)` so that a partial completion still produces a balanced audit. UUID per run: `tgt-{DRD2|JNK3|GSK3β}_seed-{0|1|2}_arm-{A0..A6|s_A|s_B|s_C}`.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Audit log schema (per-batch dump)\n\nStored as `run_id/iteration_NNNN.json`:\n\n```\n{\n  \"iter\": 4242,\n  \"run_id\": \"tgt-DRD2_seed-0_arm-A4\",\n  \"phase\": \"REINFORCE\",\n  \"sigma_ema\": 87.4,\n  \"loss\": -132.1,\n  \"loss_sampling\": -210.0,\n  \"loss_aug_lik\":  77.9,\n  \"lr\": 4.85e-05,\n  \"batch\": {\n    \"n_valid\":    241,\n    \"n_invalid\":   15,\n    \"n_seed_intact\": 232,\n    \"%invalid\":    5.86,\n    \"%seed_intact\": 90.6\n  },\n  \"reward_components\": {\n    \"S_mean\":   1.012,\n    \"S_std\":    0.21,\n    \"qed_mean\":   0.74,\n    \"plogp_mean\": 4.51,\n    \"aff_pred_mean\": 0.61,\n    \"div_bonus_mean\": 0.06\n  },\n  \"diversity_drift\": {\n    \"int_div_1_minus_tani\":       0.812,\n    \"nn_Tani_train_mean\":         0.41,\n    \"frac_long_alkane_ge8\":       0.018,\n    \"unique_murcko_scaffolds_k\":  412\n  },\n  \"validity_breakdown\": {\n    \"valency_violations\":  6,\n    \"kekulization_errors\": 4,\n    \"ring_too_large_8plus\": 2,\n    \"parse_errors\":        3\n  },\n  \"held_out_200\": {\n    \"S_rolling_mean_50\": 1.108,\n    \"S_rolling_std_50\":  0.04\n  }\n}\n```\n\n`%molecules_with_scaffold_seed_intact` is verified by SFIT substructure match (or Morgan-substructure Tanimoto ≥ 0.7 if isomorphism is non-trivial).\n\n---\n\n## 9. Stopping criterion\n\nFor every arm-seed-target run, two stop conditions (whichever first):\n\n1. **Plateau.** Rolling mean of `S_default` over the held-out 200-molecule batch is within ε = 0.01 of its previous-window value for **N = 50** consecutive update steps (~ 50 × 256 = 12 800 SMILES evaluated *post-hoc* but sampled sequentially).\n2. **Budget.** 5 000 REINFORCE updates exhausted (sweep variant: 2 000).\n\nThe held-out 200-mol batch is resampled once per 5 update steps from π_θ (T=1.0); it is *not* used for training (no leakage into σ or buffer). This is the Blaschke 2020 protocol.\n\nA1, A2, A3 stop on the same metric — minimised one-dimensional reward.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Sensitivity sweep (DRD2 only)\n\nThree weight points on A4 / DRD2 / single seed=0:\n- s_A `flat {1, 1, 1}`\n- s_B `default {0.5, 0.5, 0.8}` (the design default)\n- s_C `affinity-heavy {0.3, 0.3, 1.0}`\n\nReporting: differences ΔQED, Δp-logP, Δaff_pred, Δinternal diversity and Δ%long-alkane between s_A–s_B–s_C, with the same audit-log fixtures. Decision rule (pre-registered): if Δ across the three points is within 1 σ of the seed-noise floor (estimated from 3 seeds on s_B), prefer s_B as canonical for the main grid run; otherwise report two reference variants in §12.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Pre-registered reporting plan\n\n### Primary endpoints (drive H1, H3)\n| Metric | Group | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| QED held-out (RF-on-evaluation set) | (b) Property | ↑ |\n| Penalized logP held-out | (b) Property | ↑ |\n| Aff_pred held-out (RF) | (b) Property | ↑ |\n| Aff_pred held-out (GNN, A7) | (b) Property | ↑ |\n| Internal diversity (1−Tani) | (c) Diversity | ≥A0 |\n\n### Hallucination endpoints (drive H2)\n| Metric | Group | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| % invalid SMILES (RDKit) | (a) | ↓ |\n| Valency violation rate | (a) | ↓ |\n| Kekulization / ring-size error rate | (a) | ↓ |\n| **% molecules with contiguous sp3-C ≥ 8** | (c) | ↓ |\n| % scaffold seed intact | (a) sanity | ≥95% |\n| % Murcko scaffolds not in train | (c) | reportable; not direction by default |\n\n### Secondary / sanity endpoints\n| Metric | Group |\n|---|---|\n| Validity (MOSES @1 k, @10 k) | (a) |\n| Uniqueness @1 k, @10 k | (a) |\n| Novelty at Tani ≤ 0.4 | (a) |\n| PAINS / Brenk alert count | (a) |\n| Nearest-neighbour Tani to train (mean, 10-th percentile) | (c) |\n| # unique Murcko scaffolds | (c) |\n| Scaffold entropy | (c) |\n| Surrogate–GNN gap (A7) — measurement of OOD drift | (b) audit |\n| Random-feedback divergence from A0 (A6 − A0) | control |\n\n### Reporting tables (planned)\n- **Table 1 — Property lift:** mean ± 95 % CI per (target, arm) over 3 seeds. Difference A4 − A0 with paired-seed t-test.\n- **Table 2 — Hallucination reduction:** same grid; pre-registered Δ on %invalid and %long-alkane.\n- **Table 3 — Diversity preservation:** internal diversity, scaffold entropy, NN-Tani per arm.\n- **Table 4 — Sensitivity sweep (DRD2):** comparison s_A / s_B / s_C.\n- **Table 5 — Surrogate audit (A7):** RF vs GNN on stratified 200-subset, Pearson r + calibration plot.\n- **Table 6 — Random-feedback control (A6):** A6 vs A0 deltas; expected non-significant.\n\n### Visualisations (planned, not produced)\n- Reward-component boxplots per arm-target.\n- Internal-diversity vs composite-reward Pareto front.\n- Murcko scaffold t-SNE (generated samples vs train) coloured by `S_default`.\n- %long-alkane trajectory per step (should monotonically drop for A2/A4/A5).\n\n---\n\n## 12. Threats to validity & confounds\n\n- **Surrogate–reality gap (binding).** Aff_pred from the RF-ECFP may not rank real DRD2 affinity correctly. Mitigated by A7 (GNN re-scoring) and by reporting surrogate–GNN Pearson r; **not** by silent use of docking per-step (which destroys the feedback-loop isolation we are testing).\n- **Target embedding leakage.** Frozen ESM-2 embeddings are target-specific — if arms are too close in embedding space, conditioning effect vanishes. Confirmed distinct: P14416 / P52333 / P49841 are very dissimilar in sequence.\n- **Scaffold-seed bias.** Using a single active seed per target couples arms A1–A6 in scaffold space. Acceptable for *internal* comparison (arms use same seed), problematic for *external* comparison to literature; reported in the discussion.\n- **Composite-reward scale drift.** σ EMA can mask genuine learning vs runaway σ. Audit log keeps σ-EMA visible; reporting includes unfactored A4 − A0 with raw S.\n- **Determinism.** We seed NumPy/PyTorch/RF/Scikit-learn; report seed-level standard deviation, not collapsed mean.\n- **Random-feedback control A6** is essential — without it, monotonic reward improvement in A4 could be attributed to gradient noise on a finite sample.\n- **CPU-only sandbox cannot run the experiment** — explicit acknowledgement (§13). Numbers reported will be from the targeted reference cluster, not from the sandbox.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Feasibility in the current environment\n\n| Capability | This sandbox | Required for real run |\n|---|---|---|\n| CPU cores | 24 | ≥ 24 OK |\n| GPU | **none** | ≥ 1 × A100 (40 GB) or equivalent |\n| RAM | unknown — assume ≥ 64 GB OK | ≥ 128 GB to run RF + RF+ESM concurrently |\n| Network | **disabled** | required for ESM-2 checkpoint, ChEMBL / PDBbind / MOSES / GuacaMol downloads, DUD-E |\n| Pre-installed Python packages | system only | RDKit 2024.x, PyTorch + CUDA, PyG, scikit-learn, transformers (ESM-2), Moses benchmark scripts |\n\n**Conclusion.** This experiment is *designed but not run* in the current environment. The sandbox can be used for design review, audit-log spec validation and unit tests of the reward function (e.g., spot-checking alkane-penalty edge cases). A real run requires (i) a GPU instance, (ii) network access to the listed datasets/checkpoints, (iii) pre-installed chemistry stack. The full real-run footprint is ≈ 1 A100-month including pre-training; reduced proportionally if a public REINVENT prior checkpoint is network-loaded — which the sandbox cannot do due to network restrictions.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Decision points & remaining uncertainties\n\n- **D1.** The exact active molecule chosen as the per-target scaffold seed. Researcher to confirm whether to pick the highest-pKi ChEMBL active, or a more \"drug-like\" mid-affinity one. Effect: appears in every A1–A6 result; affects external comparability only.\n- **D2.** Threshold K = 8 for \"long alkane\" (researcher-locked) and penalty weight β = 1.0 (process default, not researcher-locked).\n- **D3.** Whether DUD-E decoys vs randomized ChEMBL inactives — currently DUD-E per researcher Q2; DUD-E is the more conservative option (matched physico-chemistry).\n- **D4.** The π_prior — release checkpoint from MolecularAI's REINVENT vs MOSES retrain. If released checkpoint is used (network permitting), pre-training step is skipped; otherwise ≈ 1 A100-day. Process decision, not scientific.\n- **D5.** Final reporting format (paper section draft vs internal report). Out of scope for design.\n\nNothing else is blocking `approve_approach`.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Repository observations\n\n- `/workspace` contains a single file: `task.json`. Confirmed by `ls -la` and recursive `find`.\n- The `documents.track.md` field is a single-line directive, included inside `task.json` (not as a separate file). The directive is treated as authoritative: design-only.\n- No source code, no checkpoints, no datasets, no logs in the workspace.\n- Network disabled, no GPU — see §13.\n- `researcher_topics` is `[]`; no pre-seeded clarifications.\n\n---\n\n## 16. One-line summary\n\nDesign registers a REINVENT-style SMILES-LM refined by a 1 k rejection warm-up + 2–5 k REINFORCE updates under the composite reward `0.5·QED + 0.5·norm(p-logP-with-alkane-penalty) + 0.8·tanh(3·aff_RF)`, evaluated on DRD2/JNK3/GSK3β with primary endpoints in property lift, hallucination reduction, and diversity preservation, with a random-feedback control and GNN oracle audit (A7) as mandatory controls; the experiment requires ~1 A100-month of GPU compute plus network access and therefore will not be executed in the current sandbox.\n", "evidence": "Repository inspection (/workspace): only /workspace/task.json is present (verified via ls -la and find -maxdepth 3 -type f). The documents.track.md field inside task.json contains a single line: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No other files exist in /workspace. No source, checkpoints, datasets or logs shipped. researcher_topics is []. Environment per task.json: 24 vCPU, no GPU, network disabled, system Python only. Per researcher direction this is design-only, experiment not executed. All numerical anchors (MOSES sizes, sigma in [60,120], RF n_estimators=1000, Morgan r=2 2048 b, K=8 alkane, etc.) are public-literature references or researcher-confirmed defaults — none are claimed as experimental outcomes.", "assumptions": ["A1. Three feedback signals: QED, p-logP, binding affinity only (no separate SA/ringP weight channels beyond their role inside p-logP).", "A2. Long-alkane penalty is additive INSIDE p-logP (alongside SA and ringP), not a separate weighted reward component. Threshold K=8 contiguous sp3-C.", "A3. Default reward weights 0.5*QED + 0.5*norm(p-logP clipped to [-5,+10]) + 0.8*tanh(3*aff_RF).", "A4. Targets DRD2 (CHEMBL217 / P14416), JNK3 (CHEMBL1936 / P52333), GSK3beta (CHEMBL262 / P49841); 'active' = IC50/Ki <= 1 muM (REINVENT 2.0 convention); decoys = DUD-E per target.", "A5. DRD2-vs-DRD3 selectivity is an extra eval metric on the DRD2 arm only, not a separate training arm; DRD3 oracle is computed at evaluation time only.", "A6. Affinity oracle inside RL loop = per-target RandomForestRegressor on Morgan r=2 ECFP, 2048 bits, n_estimators=1000, n_jobs=-1, max_features='sqrt'.", "A7. A7 is re-scoring only with a pretrained GNN affinity model (DimeNet or PyG-equivalent), on a stratified ~200-molecule subset per (arm, target, seed).", "A8. Per-arm compute budget = 1 k rejection warm-up + 2-5 k REINFORCE updates + 10 k evaluation molecules.", "A9. Per-molecule scalar reward at end of trajectory; no distributional RL.", "A10. A0 unconditional prior-only SMILES; A1-A6 target-conditioned with frozen ESM-2 (650M) embedding + identical per-target scaffold seed across arms and seeds.", "A11. Beta=1.0 weight for alkane-penalty term inside p-logP (process default, not researcher-locked).", "A12. DimeNet/GNN implementation obtained via public PyG codebase for A7.", "A13. Sampling temperature T = 1.0 throughout; no temperature annealing.", "A14. Aggregate ~720 GPU-h grid is a planning figure only, not a runtime claim.", "A15. The sandbox (CPU-only, no network) is acknowledged unsuitable for execution; the design is the deliverable.", "A16. Same held-out 200-molecule stopping-batch is used across all arms of a single run but seeded independently across runs."], "uncertainties": ["U1. Which exact molecule to use as the per-target scaffold seed (highest-pKi ChEMBL active vs more drug-like mid-affinity).", "U2. Which ESM-2 size to use (650M default; 8B exists but increases GPU RAM).", "U3. Whether to load a public MolecularAI REINVENT prior checkpoint (network permitting) or retrain on MOSES in-house.", "U4. Whether PAINS / Brenk alert counts should be primary or secondary endpoints.", "U5. Stratification rule for the A7 200-molecule subset (by composite-reward deciles vs by qed / p-logP / affinity quantiles).", "U6. Whether to report a 3-target aggregate in addition to per-target tables in Table 1.", "U7. Tie-breaking rule when sigma*S is equal across buffer candidates (epsilon-noise vs strict ordering).", "U8. Choice of alkane-penalty beta within p-logP (currently beta=1.0; not researcher-locked)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-6d7f8437-7d71-482d-b4a2-213ff6343471", "tool_call": {"id": "call-6d7f8437-7d71-482d-b4a2-213ff6343471", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full design: rationale, controls, analysis, feasibility\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"\\n# Full experiment design \\u2014 Chemical feedback loop in targeted molecule discovery\\n*Design-only; no experiment executed. All numerical anchors are public-literature references, not results.*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Pre-registered research questions & hypotheses\\n\\n**RQ.** Does an explicit feedback loop \\u2014 QED + penalized logP + binding-affinity surrogate \\u2014 in generator fine-tuning:\\n- (a) improve property scores on the held-out evaluator,\\n- (b) reduce \\\"molecular hallucinations\\\" (notably ultra-long sp3-C chains, valency errors, generation collapse to top-K scaffolds),\\n- (c) preserve structural diversity relative to the unconditional prior.\\n\\n**H1 (property lift).** A4 (full triplet) attains higher held-out QED, p-logP and affinity than A0 (prior only) with p < 0.05 across 3 seeds.\\n**H2 (hallucination reduction).** A4 reduces % molecules with \\u2265 8 contiguous sp3-C and % invalid SMILES vs A0.\\n**H3 (diversity preserved).** A5 (A4 + diversity bonus) is non-inferior to A4 on internal diversity; A4 + diversity bonus is non-inferior to A0 on internal diversity and unique-Murcko-scaffold count.\\n**H4 (signal specificity).** A6 (random-feedback) shows no significant improvement over A0 on any property \\u2014 establishes that gains are signal-driven, not noise-driven.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Base generator architecture\\n\\n**Choice (confirmed): REINVENT-style SMILES-LM** \\u2014 RNN/Self-attention encoder\\u2013decoder over canonical SMILES with start/stop tokens, ~3\\u20136 M parameters (range consistent with Olivecrona 2017 / Blaschke 2020 prior networks).\\n\\n- **Prior \\u03c0_prior.** Pre-trained on MOSES 1.59 M SMILES (string-only prior, no properties) \\u2014 used by all arms.\\n- **Policy \\u03c0_\\u03b8.** Initialized from \\u03c0_prior; updated by either rejection warm-up (\\u00a73.1) or REINFORCE (\\u00a73.2).\\n- **Tokenization.** Atom-wise SMILES with a small atom-vocab + ring-bond markers; same vocab as Prior; max length 128 tokens (MOSES convention; 99th-percentile of training set is \\u2248 74 tokens).\\n- **Chemistry validity is not enforced at decode time** \\u2014 the feedback signal is the only validity pressure (so we can measure the hallucination frequency).\\n\\nWhy over JT-VAE / GraphAF / DiffuMol: REINVENT-style aug-likelihood REINFORCE accepts per-molecule scalar rewards natively, supports MCS-conditioned seeding, and has the most mature public reporting for the same targets (DRD2/JNK3/GSK3\\u03b2) \\u2014 direct comparability with public benchmarks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Feedback signals \\u2014 exact formulations\\n\\nAll three signals are computed per generated SMILES and aggregated into a scalar reward S(m).\\n\\n### 2.1 QED\\nCompute with RDKit `QED.qed(m)` (Bickerton et al. 2012). Default range [0, 1].\\n\\n### 2.2 Penalized logP (with embedded long-alkane penalty, no extra weight)\\nPer researcher direction (Q1): long-alkane penalty is additive *inside* p-logP, alongside the SA and ringP terms.\\n\\n```\\npLogP(m) = clip(logP_Crippen(m), -5, +10)\\n         - SA(m)                                          # Ertl & Schuffenhauer, 2009\\n         - max(0, 6 - n_rings(m))                         # ring penalty, Kusner 2017\\n         - alkane_penalty(m, K=8, beta=1.0)               # NEW: contiguous sp3-C >= K\\n```\\n\\nWhere `alkane_penalty(m, K, \\u03b2) = \\u03b2 \\u00b7 max(0, L_longest_sp3_C_chain(m) \\u2212 K + 1)`. With K=8 the penalty ramps linearly for chain lengths \\u2265 9.\\n\\nThe composite p-logP is normalised into [0,1] by `norm_pLogP = (pLogP \\u2212 (-5)) / (10 \\u2212 (-5))`-then-clipped; this matches `Q1`'s call for `norm(p-logP in [-5,+10])`.\\n\\n### 2.3 Binding-affinity surrogate\\nPer-target **RF-ECFP** regressor pKi:\\n- Features: Morgan ECFP, radius 2, 2048 bits, byte-packed.\\n- Model: `sklearn.ensemble.RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=1000, n_jobs=-1, max_features=\\\"sqrt\\\", random_state=seed)` \\u2014 per researcher Q3.\\n- Training data per target: ChEMBL actives (IC50/Ki \\u2264 1 \\u00b5M, REINVENT-2.0 convention) \\u222a DUD-E decoys for that target, both refolded to pKi (= 9 \\u2212 log10(IC50_M)); 80/10/10 split; reported RMSE on 10 % holdout and held-back re-scoring set.\\n- Surrogate output: `aff_pred = clip(pKi/10, 0, 1)` (pKi \\u2208 [0,10] \\u2192 [0,1]).\\n- Surrogate score passed into the reward: `tanh(3 \\u00b7 aff_pred)` for finite-range robustness (so a saturated hit does not indefinitely dominate the gradient).\\n\\n### 2.4 Composite reward (researcher-confirmed default)\\n```\\nS_default(m) = 0.5 \\u00b7 QED(m)\\n             + 0.5 \\u00b7 norm_pLogP(m)\\n             + 0.8 \\u00b7 tanh(3 \\u00b7 aff_pred(m))\\n```\\nRange S \\u2208 [0, ~2.1]; not normalised \\u2014 REINVENT aug-likelihood operates on raw S with a `sigma` scaler (\\u00a73.2).\\n\\nFor A5 the diversity bonus `\\u03bb_div \\u00b7 (1/|B|) \\u00b7 \\u03a3_{i\\u2260j in B}(1 \\u2212 Tani(i,j))` is added inside S (see \\u00a73.3).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Feedback-loop mechanism\\n\\n### 3.1 Rejection-sampling warm-up (1 k molecules by researcher budget)\\n- Sample 100 000 SMILES from \\u03c0_prior with T=1.0; canonicalize with RDKit.\\n- Compute S_default(m) for each.\\n- Keep top-k (k = 1 000 by default), and use these as *teacher data* for one-shot supervised fine-tuning of \\u03c0_\\u03b8 (cross-entropy on token sequences). This is SST \\u2014 REINVENT 2.0 / Blaschke 2020.\\n- Goal: move \\u03c0_\\u03b8 away from the prior enough that the policy-gradient phase has nonzero reward variance without diverging.\\n\\n### 3.2 REINFORCE with augmented likelihood (2\\u20135 k updates by researcher budget)\\n- Sample a batch of B = 256 SMILES from \\u03c0_\\u03b8 (T=1.0) at each step.\\n- For each valid SMILES m, compute S(m). Invalid SMILES receive S = S_invalid (configurable but set to 0 by default).\\n- Augmented-likelihood loss per step (REINVENT 3.0):\\n\\n```\\nL(\\u03b8) = -E_{m ~ \\u03c0_\\u03b8}[ \\u03a3_t log \\u03c0_\\u03b8(a_t | a_<t, cond)  \\u00b7  cloze_factor   ]\\n        - \\u03c3 \\u00b7 S(m)\\n```\\n\\nwhere \\u03c3 is a learned scaler (REINVENT-style scalar \\\"sigma\\\", initialised at 60\\u2013120; updated via a moving-average of |L| to keep the gradient contributions balanced). `cloze_factor` follows Blaschke 2020 (down-weights tokens completed long after average length to avoid bias toward short molecules).\\n\\n- Experience-replay buffer (\\u00a75) feeds 25 % of each batch from replay and 75 % from fresh sampling.\\n- LR scheduler: linear warm-up 1 k steps, cosine decay to 10 % by step 5 k. \\u03c3 updates every 50 steps.\\n\\n### 3.3 Diversity-preservation term (A5 only)\\nAt REINFORCE step end, form S'(m) by adding to S_default(m) a per-batch diversity bonus:\\n\\n```\\nS_A5(m) = S_default(m) + \\u03bb_div \\u00b7 (1/\\u2212Tani_vs_batch_mean(m))\\n```\\n\\n`\\u03bb_div` initial value = 0.2, annealed linearly to 0.05 by step 5 k. Diversity is computed over the *batch* (cheap; per-batch, not per-experience). Effect: discourages within-batch collapse to identical Murcko scaffolds.\\n\\n### 3.4 Why not pure rejection sampling, constrained decoding, or distributional RL?\\n- *Pure RS* (no REINFORCE): saturates at \\u03c0_prior's modifiable support, weak DRD2-conditioning signal because the prior already produces drug-like molecules with random target affinity.\\n- *Constrained decoding* (e.g., on scaffold alone): cannot enforce QED/affinity at per-token granularity without an expensive search oracle per token.\\n- *Distributional RL / per-token credit:* increases variance for a relatively short trajectory (\\u2264 128 tokens), and we already have scaffold seed + augment-likelihood smoothing; user direction (Q5) is per-molecule scalar.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Datasets & conditioning\\n\\n| Role | Source | Size | Use |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Pre-training | MOSES (Polykovskiy 2020) | 1.59 M SMILES | \\u03c0_prior |\\n| Distribution eval | MOSES test + GuacaMol 10 K | ~120 k + 10 k | validity/uniqueness/novelty @1 k & @10 k |\\n| Target fine-tune | ChEMBL28 filtered: DRD2 (CHEMBL217), JNK3 (CHEMBL1936), GSK3\\u03b2 (CHEMBL262) | IC50/Ki \\u2264 1 \\u00b5M | per-target RF training + policy-conditioning head |\\n| Target decoys | DUD-E for the same three targets | DUD-E provided | per-target RF training (1:1 active:decoy) |\\n| Affinity ground-truth refr. | PDBbind-refined 2020 | \\u2248 5 K complexes | held-out surrogate re-fitter (NOT used to train RF in RL loop \\u2014 only to sanity-check A7) |\\n| Conditioning embedding | ESM-2 (650 M params, frozen) over UniProt sequences P14416 (DRD2), P52333 (JNK3 / MAPK10), P49841 (GSK3\\u03b2) | 3 embeddings | injected into decoder init for A1\\u2013A6 |\\n| Scaffold seeds | One known ChEMBL active per target, fixed across arms | 3 seeds | implanted as required substructure by decoder mask |\\n\\n**A0 \\u2014 unconditional** (no conditioning embedding, no scaffold seed).\\n**A1\\u2013A6 \\u2014 target-conditioned** with the same per-target active seed (one molecule per target, chosen for being a strong literature active, used identically across seeds and arms so diversity claims are not confounded by scaffold choice).\\n**A7 \\u2014 re-scoring only**, not a training arm.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Hyperparameters & compute footprint\\n\\n### 5.1 Per-arm hyperparameters (frozen across seeds)\\n| Param | Value |\\n|---|---|\\n| Batch size B | 256 |\\n| Warm-up size | 100 000 sampled \\u2192 1 000 kept |\\n| REINFORCE updates | 2 000 (sensitivity sweep) / 5 000 (main grid); capped |\\n| Optimizer | Adam, lr = 1e-4 (warm-up); 5e-5 (REINFORCE) |\\n| \\u03c3 (REINVENT scaler) | init 100, EMA update \\u03b2=0.95 |\\n| Replay buffer size | 5 000 molecules (ring buffer; refresh policy below) |\\n| Replay sampling fraction | 25 % per batch |\\n| Replay refresh | when buffer full: keep the 80 % with highest \\u03c3\\u00b7S among incoming, evict the rest (MoriaRE-style) |\\n| Aug-likelihood cloze factor | linear, 0.5 at t=T/2, 1.0 at t=T |\\n| Target seed for training subset | fixed (per target, opt-in by user) |\\n\\n### 5.2 Compute footprint\\n**In the present sandbox (24 vCPU, no GPU, network disabled):** *the experiment cannot be run as defined.* Reasons: SMILES-LM training and ESM-2 inference benefit materially from a GPU; both the MOSES pre-training and ChEMBL filtering require network I/O not available. The sandbox is therefore used only for design \\u2014 pre-registered specs, dry-run scripts, and unit-level probes (e.g., reward-function sanity tests) where possible.\\n\\n**CPU-only footprint estimate (per arm \\u00d7 per seed), if executed as a CPU-only feasibility prep:**\\n- \\u03c0_prior (frozen, no training): ~0 CPU-h (just loading weights).\\n- Surrogate RF training: ~10\\u201320 CPU-h per target (randomforest 1000 trees, 2048-bit features, ~10\\u201330 K training rows).\\n- Surrogate inference over 100 K+10 K SMILES per arm: ~3 CPU-h.\\n- RDKit property pipeline (QED/SA/p-logP/alkane scan): ~1 CPU-h.\\n- ESM-2 embedding of 3 targets (frozen): trivial, ~0.1 CPU-h.\\n- REINVENT REINFORCE loop (5 000 updates \\u00d7 batch 256): **GPU-bound**, CPU-only estimate \\u2248 30\\u201350 CPU-h per arm \\u00d7 seed at ~1 batch / 30 s; reproduce-without-GPU would be impractically slow (\\u2248 2 weeks wall-clock per single arm-seed-target on a 24-vCPU box).\\n- Evaluation: ~0.2 CPU-h per 10 K SMILES re-scoring.\\n\\n**Equivalent GPU footprint** (researcher-relevant, for a single A100/40 GB):\\n- \\u03c0_prior fine-tune / SST warm-up: ~30 min.\\n- REINFORCE: ~6\\u201310 h per arm \\u00d7 seed-target on A100.\\n- Surrogate & property pipelines: trivial.\\n- **Aggregate grid** = 3 targets \\u00d7 7 arms \\u00d7 3 seeds + 3 sensitivity points (DRD2 only) + A7 re-scoring overhead = **72 REINVENT runs \\u00d7 ~10 GPU-h \\u2248 720 GPU-h \\u2248 1 A100-month.** Plus pre-training (~1 A100-day if retraining, ~0 A100-day if released checkpoints are network-loaded \\u2014 only the latter is realistic in a CPU+offline sandbox).\\n- Storage: ~30 GB weights + ~10 GB datasets.\\n\\n### 5.3 Replay buffer\\nRing buffer of 5 000 molecules (per arm). Refresh policy: when buffer full, score incoming molecules with \\u03c3\\u00b7S, replace the lowest-scoring quartile. Sampling: 25 % of every batch drawn uniformly from the buffer, 75 % drawn from the current \\u03c0_\\u03b8. Buffer state is *not* shared across arms (each arm keeps its own).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Arms (controls & ablations)\\n\\n| Arm | Feedback | Conditioning | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **A0** | none | unconditional | prior-only samples, baseline for property/diversity |\\n| **A1** | QED | target-conditioned | single-property isolation |\\n| **A2** | p-logP (with embedded alkane penalty) | target-conditioned | isolates QED-vs-p-logP contribution |\\n| **A3** | affinity (per-target RF-ECFP) | target-conditioned | isolates affinity contribution |\\n| **A4** | QED + p-logP + affinity | target-conditioned | **full triplet**, default weights 0.5/0.5/0.8 |\\n| **A5** | A4 + diversity bonus \\u03bb_div\\u00b7pair_div | target-conditioned | tests whether diversity degrades under feedback |\\n| **A6** | **random feedback** \\u2014 molecule\\u2192score mapping permuted inside every batch (deterministic permutation seeded by iteration) | target-conditioned | gradient noise control (cf. Blaschke 2020); must remain flat vs A0 |\\n| **A7** | re-scoring only (no training) | \\u2014 | A3/A4 re-scored by an **out-of-loop GNN affinity scorer** (DimeNet-based or equivalent, public PyG implementation, frozen) on a stratified subsample of \\u2248 200 molecules per (arm, target, seed); measures surrogate\\u2013out-of-distribution gap |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Subset ordering & execution schedule\\n\\nExecution is *embarrassingly parallel across arms \\u00d7 seeds \\u00d7 targets* but is sequenced to keep the audit matrix contiguous:\\n\\n- **Phase S \\u2014 sensitivity sweep on DRD2 only** (single seed = 0 to keep cost low):\\n  - weights `flat {1, 1, 1}` (s_A), `default {0.5, 0.5, 0.8}` (s_B), `affinity-heavy {0.3, 0.3, 1.0}` (s_C)\\n  - applies **only to A4**; variants s_A, s_B, s_C\\n- **Phase G \\u2014 main grid:**\\n  - 3 targets \\u00d7 7 arms (A0\\u2013A6) \\u00d7 3 seeds = **63 runs**\\n- **Phase R \\u2014 sanity / A7 re-scoring:**\\n  - 3 targets \\u00d7 2 arms (A3, A4) \\u00d7 3 seeds = **18 runs** of re-scoring only\\n- Total runs: **3 (sweep) + 63 (grid) + 18 (A7) = 84 runs**.\\n\\nRun order: interleaved by `(seed, target, arm)` so that a partial completion still produces a balanced audit. UUID per run: `tgt-{DRD2|JNK3|GSK3\\u03b2}_seed-{0|1|2}_arm-{A0..A6|s_A|s_B|s_C}`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Audit log schema (per-batch dump)\\n\\nStored as `run_id/iteration_NNNN.json`:\\n\\n```\\n{\\n  \\\"iter\\\": 4242,\\n  \\\"run_id\\\": \\\"tgt-DRD2_seed-0_arm-A4\\\",\\n  \\\"phase\\\": \\\"REINFORCE\\\",\\n  \\\"sigma_ema\\\": 87.4,\\n  \\\"loss\\\": -132.1,\\n  \\\"loss_sampling\\\": -210.0,\\n  \\\"loss_aug_lik\\\":  77.9,\\n  \\\"lr\\\": 4.85e-05,\\n  \\\"batch\\\": {\\n    \\\"n_valid\\\":    241,\\n    \\\"n_invalid\\\":   15,\\n    \\\"n_seed_intact\\\": 232,\\n    \\\"%invalid\\\":    5.86,\\n    \\\"%seed_intact\\\": 90.6\\n  },\\n  \\\"reward_components\\\": {\\n    \\\"S_mean\\\":   1.012,\\n    \\\"S_std\\\":    0.21,\\n    \\\"qed_mean\\\":   0.74,\\n    \\\"plogp_mean\\\": 4.51,\\n    \\\"aff_pred_mean\\\": 0.61,\\n    \\\"div_bonus_mean\\\": 0.06\\n  },\\n  \\\"diversity_drift\\\": {\\n    \\\"int_div_1_minus_tani\\\":       0.812,\\n    \\\"nn_Tani_train_mean\\\":         0.41,\\n    \\\"frac_long_alkane_ge8\\\":       0.018,\\n    \\\"unique_murcko_scaffolds_k\\\":  412\\n  },\\n  \\\"validity_breakdown\\\": {\\n    \\\"valency_violations\\\":  6,\\n    \\\"kekulization_errors\\\": 4,\\n    \\\"ring_too_large_8plus\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"parse_errors\\\":        3\\n  },\\n  \\\"held_out_200\\\": {\\n    \\\"S_rolling_mean_50\\\": 1.108,\\n    \\\"S_rolling_std_50\\\":  0.04\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`%molecules_with_scaffold_seed_intact` is verified by SFIT substructure match (or Morgan-substructure Tanimoto \\u2265 0.7 if isomorphism is non-trivial).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Stopping criterion\\n\\nFor every arm-seed-target run, two stop conditions (whichever first):\\n\\n1. **Plateau.** Rolling mean of `S_default` over the held-out 200-molecule batch is within \\u03b5 = 0.01 of its previous-window value for **N = 50** consecutive update steps (~ 50 \\u00d7 256 = 12 800 SMILES evaluated *post-hoc* but sampled sequentially).\\n2. **Budget.** 5 000 REINFORCE updates exhausted (sweep variant: 2 000).\\n\\nThe held-out 200-mol batch is resampled once per 5 update steps from \\u03c0_\\u03b8 (T=1.0); it is *not* used for training (no leakage into \\u03c3 or buffer). This is the Blaschke 2020 protocol.\\n\\nA1, A2, A3 stop on the same metric \\u2014 minimised one-dimensional reward.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Sensitivity sweep (DRD2 only)\\n\\nThree weight points on A4 / DRD2 / single seed=0:\\n- s_A `flat {1, 1, 1}`\\n- s_B `default {0.5, 0.5, 0.8}` (the design default)\\n- s_C `affinity-heavy {0.3, 0.3, 1.0}`\\n\\nReporting: differences \\u0394QED, \\u0394p-logP, \\u0394aff_pred, \\u0394internal diversity and \\u0394%long-alkane between s_A\\u2013s_B\\u2013s_C, with the same audit-log fixtures. Decision rule (pre-registered): if \\u0394 across the three points is within 1 \\u03c3 of the seed-noise floor (estimated from 3 seeds on s_B), prefer s_B as canonical for the main grid run; otherwise report two reference variants in \\u00a712.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Pre-registered reporting plan\\n\\n### Primary endpoints (drive H1, H3)\\n| Metric | Group | Direction |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| QED held-out (RF-on-evaluation set) | (b) Property | \\u2191 |\\n| Penalized logP held-out | (b) Property | \\u2191 |\\n| Aff_pred held-out (RF) | (b) Property | \\u2191 |\\n| Aff_pred held-out (GNN, A7) | (b) Property | \\u2191 |\\n| Internal diversity (1\\u2212Tani) | (c) Diversity | \\u2265A0 |\\n\\n### Hallucination endpoints (drive H2)\\n| Metric | Group | Direction |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| % invalid SMILES (RDKit) | (a) | \\u2193 |\\n| Valency violation rate | (a) | \\u2193 |\\n| Kekulization / ring-size error rate | (a) | \\u2193 |\\n| **% molecules with contiguous sp3-C \\u2265 8** | (c) | \\u2193 |\\n| % scaffold seed intact | (a) sanity | \\u226595% |\\n| % Murcko scaffolds not in train | (c) | reportable; not direction by default |\\n\\n### Secondary / sanity endpoints\\n| Metric | Group |\\n|---|---|\\n| Validity (MOSES @1 k, @10 k) | (a) |\\n| Uniqueness @1 k, @10 k | (a) |\\n| Novelty at Tani \\u2264 0.4 | (a) |\\n| PAINS / Brenk alert count | (a) |\\n| Nearest-neighbour Tani to train (mean, 10-th percentile) | (c) |\\n| # unique Murcko scaffolds | (c) |\\n| Scaffold entropy | (c) |\\n| Surrogate\\u2013GNN gap (A7) \\u2014 measurement of OOD drift | (b) audit |\\n| Random-feedback divergence from A0 (A6 \\u2212 A0) | control |\\n\\n### Reporting tables (planned)\\n- **Table 1 \\u2014 Property lift:** mean \\u00b1 95 % CI per (target, arm) over 3 seeds. Difference A4 \\u2212 A0 with paired-seed t-test.\\n- **Table 2 \\u2014 Hallucination reduction:** same grid; pre-registered \\u0394 on %invalid and %long-alkane.\\n- **Table 3 \\u2014 Diversity preservation:** internal diversity, scaffold entropy, NN-Tani per arm.\\n- **Table 4 \\u2014 Sensitivity sweep (DRD2):** comparison s_A / s_B / s_C.\\n- **Table 5 \\u2014 Surrogate audit (A7):** RF vs GNN on stratified 200-subset, Pearson r + calibration plot.\\n- **Table 6 \\u2014 Random-feedback control (A6):** A6 vs A0 deltas; expected non-significant.\\n\\n### Visualisations (planned, not produced)\\n- Reward-component boxplots per arm-target.\\n- Internal-diversity vs composite-reward Pareto front.\\n- Murcko scaffold t-SNE (generated samples vs train) coloured by `S_default`.\\n- %long-alkane trajectory per step (should monotonically drop for A2/A4/A5).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Threats to validity & confounds\\n\\n- **Surrogate\\u2013reality gap (binding).** Aff_pred from the RF-ECFP may not rank real DRD2 affinity correctly. Mitigated by A7 (GNN re-scoring) and by reporting surrogate\\u2013GNN Pearson r; **not** by silent use of docking per-step (which destroys the feedback-loop isolation we are testing).\\n- **Target embedding leakage.** Frozen ESM-2 embeddings are target-specific \\u2014 if arms are too close in embedding space, conditioning effect vanishes. Confirmed distinct: P14416 / P52333 / P49841 are very dissimilar in sequence.\\n- **Scaffold-seed bias.** Using a single active seed per target couples arms A1\\u2013A6 in scaffold space. Acceptable for *internal* comparison (arms use same seed), problematic for *external* comparison to literature; reported in the discussion.\\n- **Composite-reward scale drift.** \\u03c3 EMA can mask genuine learning vs runaway \\u03c3. Audit log keeps \\u03c3-EMA visible; reporting includes unfactored A4 \\u2212 A0 with raw S.\\n- **Determinism.** We seed NumPy/PyTorch/RF/Scikit-learn; report seed-level standard deviation, not collapsed mean.\\n- **Random-feedback control A6** is essential \\u2014 without it, monotonic reward improvement in A4 could be attributed to gradient noise on a finite sample.\\n- **CPU-only sandbox cannot run the experiment** \\u2014 explicit acknowledgement (\\u00a713). Numbers reported will be from the targeted reference cluster, not from the sandbox.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 13. Feasibility in the current environment\\n\\n| Capability | This sandbox | Required for real run |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| CPU cores | 24 | \\u2265 24 OK |\\n| GPU | **none** | \\u2265 1 \\u00d7 A100 (40 GB) or equivalent |\\n| RAM | unknown \\u2014 assume \\u2265 64 GB OK | \\u2265 128 GB to run RF + RF+ESM concurrently |\\n| Network | **disabled** | required for ESM-2 checkpoint, ChEMBL / PDBbind / MOSES / GuacaMol downloads, DUD-E |\\n| Pre-installed Python packages | system only | RDKit 2024.x, PyTorch + CUDA, PyG, scikit-learn, transformers (ESM-2), Moses benchmark scripts |\\n\\n**Conclusion.** This experiment is *designed but not run* in the current environment. The sandbox can be used for design review, audit-log spec validation and unit tests of the reward function (e.g., spot-checking alkane-penalty edge cases). A real run requires (i) a GPU instance, (ii) network access to the listed datasets/checkpoints, (iii) pre-installed chemistry stack. The full real-run footprint is \\u2248 1 A100-month including pre-training; reduced proportionally if a public REINVENT prior checkpoint is network-loaded \\u2014 which the sandbox cannot do due to network restrictions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 14. Decision points & remaining uncertainties\\n\\n- **D1.** The exact active molecule chosen as the per-target scaffold seed. Researcher to confirm whether to pick the highest-pKi ChEMBL active, or a more \\\"drug-like\\\" mid-affinity one. Effect: appears in every A1\\u2013A6 result; affects external comparability only.\\n- **D2.** Threshold K = 8 for \\\"long alkane\\\" (researcher-locked) and penalty weight \\u03b2 = 1.0 (process default, not researcher-locked).\\n- **D3.** Whether DUD-E decoys vs randomized ChEMBL inactives \\u2014 currently DUD-E per researcher Q2; DUD-E is the more conservative option (matched physico-chemistry).\\n- **D4.** The \\u03c0_prior \\u2014 release checkpoint from MolecularAI's REINVENT vs MOSES retrain. If released checkpoint is used (network permitting), pre-training step is skipped; otherwise \\u2248 1 A100-day. Process decision, not scientific.\\n- **D5.** Final reporting format (paper section draft vs internal report). Out of scope for design.\\n\\nNothing else is blocking `approve_approach`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 15. Repository observations\\n\\n- `/workspace` contains a single file: `task.json`. Confirmed by `ls -la` and recursive `find`.\\n- The `documents.track.md` field is a single-line directive, included inside `task.json` (not as a separate file). The directive is treated as authoritative: design-only.\\n- No source code, no checkpoints, no datasets, no logs in the workspace.\\n- Network disabled, no GPU \\u2014 see \\u00a713.\\n- `researcher_topics` is `[]`; no pre-seeded clarifications.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 16. One-line summary\\n\\nDesign registers a REINVENT-style SMILES-LM refined by a 1 k rejection warm-up + 2\\u20135 k REINFORCE updates under the composite reward `0.5\\u00b7QED + 0.5\\u00b7norm(p-logP-with-alkane-penalty) + 0.8\\u00b7tanh(3\\u00b7aff_RF)`, evaluated on DRD2/JNK3/GSK3\\u03b2 with primary endpoints in property lift, hallucination reduction, and diversity preservation, with a random-feedback control and GNN oracle audit (A7) as mandatory controls; the experiment requires ~1 A100-month of GPU compute plus network access and therefore will not be executed in the current sandbox.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"Repository inspection (/workspace): only /workspace/task.json is present (verified via ls -la and find -maxdepth 3 -type f). The documents.track.md field inside task.json contains a single line: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No other files exist in /workspace. No source, checkpoints, datasets or logs shipped. researcher_topics is []. Environment per task.json: 24 vCPU, no GPU, network disabled, system Python only. Per researcher direction this is design-only, experiment not executed. All numerical anchors (MOSES sizes, sigma in [60,120], RF n_estimators=1000, Morgan r=2 2048 b, K=8 alkane, etc.) are public-literature references or researcher-confirmed defaults \\u2014 none are claimed as experimental outcomes.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. Three feedback signals: QED, p-logP, binding affinity only (no separate SA/ringP weight channels beyond their role inside p-logP).\", \"A2. Long-alkane penalty is additive INSIDE p-logP (alongside SA and ringP), not a separate weighted reward component. Threshold K=8 contiguous sp3-C.\", \"A3. Default reward weights 0.5*QED + 0.5*norm(p-logP clipped to [-5,+10]) + 0.8*tanh(3*aff_RF).\", \"A4. Targets DRD2 (CHEMBL217 / P14416), JNK3 (CHEMBL1936 / P52333), GSK3beta (CHEMBL262 / P49841); 'active' = IC50/Ki <= 1 muM (REINVENT 2.0 convention); decoys = DUD-E per target.\", \"A5. DRD2-vs-DRD3 selectivity is an extra eval metric on the DRD2 arm only, not a separate training arm; DRD3 oracle is computed at evaluation time only.\", \"A6. Affinity oracle inside RL loop = per-target RandomForestRegressor on Morgan r=2 ECFP, 2048 bits, n_estimators=1000, n_jobs=-1, max_features='sqrt'.\", \"A7. A7 is re-scoring only with a pretrained GNN affinity model (DimeNet or PyG-equivalent), on a stratified ~200-molecule subset per (arm, target, seed).\", \"A8. Per-arm compute budget = 1 k rejection warm-up + 2-5 k REINFORCE updates + 10 k evaluation molecules.\", \"A9. Per-molecule scalar reward at end of trajectory; no distributional RL.\", \"A10. A0 unconditional prior-only SMILES; A1-A6 target-conditioned with frozen ESM-2 (650M) embedding + identical per-target scaffold seed across arms and seeds.\", \"A11. Beta=1.0 weight for alkane-penalty term inside p-logP (process default, not researcher-locked).\", \"A12. DimeNet/GNN implementation obtained via public PyG codebase for A7.\", \"A13. Sampling temperature T = 1.0 throughout; no temperature annealing.\", \"A14. Aggregate ~720 GPU-h grid is a planning figure only, not a runtime claim.\", \"A15. The sandbox (CPU-only, no network) is acknowledged unsuitable for execution; the design is the deliverable.\", \"A16. Same held-out 200-molecule stopping-batch is used across all arms of a single run but seeded independently across runs.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1. Which exact molecule to use as the per-target scaffold seed (highest-pKi ChEMBL active vs more drug-like mid-affinity).\", \"U2. Which ESM-2 size to use (650M default; 8B exists but increases GPU RAM).\", \"U3. Whether to load a public MolecularAI REINVENT prior checkpoint (network permitting) or retrain on MOSES in-house.\", \"U4. Whether PAINS / Brenk alert counts should be primary or secondary endpoints.\", \"U5. Stratification rule for the A7 200-molecule subset (by composite-reward deciles vs by qed / p-logP / affinity quantiles).\", \"U6. Whether to report a 3-target aggregate in addition to per-target tables in Table 1.\", \"U7. Tie-breaking rule when sigma*S is equal across buffer candidates (epsilon-noise vs strict ordering).\", \"U8. Choice of alkane-penalty beta within p-logP (currently beta=1.0; not researcher-locked).\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-199fb387-faa5-477d-a63d-0d6ba29ff2d4", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
